WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
BARD SUCKS:
Google Bard sucks or atleast that seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.660
be the general opinion. Hating on Bard definitely&nbsp;
seems to be the popular thing. Twitter is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:15.180
literally filled with tweets mocking Bard for its&nbsp;
failures and it’s no wonder why. From resorting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.180
to Bing to get the answer to recommending Edge&nbsp;
over Chrome, Bard has made quite a few hilarious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.960
blunders. But I don’t this is the real problem. I&nbsp;
mean, there’s plenty of compilaations of ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:33.780
making mistakes as well. From thinking that inches&nbsp;
is a unit of mass to not knowing how to multiply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.660
negative numbers, ChatGPT is prone to making a&nbsp;
bunch of mistakes as well. But what is different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.820
between the two is how people respond to&nbsp;
the same mistakes. When Bard messes up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:50.880
people tend to laugh at it and the attitude seems&nbsp;
to carry a negative connotation. “If I were Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.580
Altman or Satya Nadella, I would be feeling VERY&nbsp;
concerned right now.” When ChatGPT messes up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:02.760
however, it tends to just become a meme. It’s the&nbsp;
classic case of people tend to laugh with ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:09.360
while they laugh at Bard. Of course, this is very&nbsp;
much anecdotal but I think there’s more to it than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.220
just that. Take a look at the adoption rates&nbsp;
for example. Bard was able to reach 30 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:20.520
users within 1 month of launch which sounds great&nbsp;
until you realize that ChatGPT was able to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.880
100 million within just 2 months. And yes, I do&nbsp;
know that Bard launched with a waiting list but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.560
I don’t think that really made a difference.&nbsp;
Just take a look at Google’s own trends data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:37.260
This becomes exponentially worse when you consider&nbsp;
that ChatGPT came from OpenAI, a no name company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:43.140
that most people have never heard of, while Bard&nbsp;
literally came directly from Google, likely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:48.240
most ubiquitous internet company in the world.&nbsp;
Clearly, there seems to be a general hesitance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:54.060
when it comes to even trying Bard. I mean think&nbsp;
about this. People are literally willing to go out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.880
of their way to use Bing and Edge to use ChatGPT&nbsp;
instead of just using Google and Chrome. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:05.400
don’t think it has anything to do with how good&nbsp;
or bad Bard actually is. I mean, you can easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.680
make a case for Bard being better. For starters,&nbsp;
it has live access to the internet, it integrates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.860
with Google, and the UI is arguably far better.&nbsp;
Yet no one seems to care and I think this has more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:23.340
to do with the Google brand as opposed to Google&nbsp;
Bard. So, join me as we examine the deterioration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.280
of the Google brand and why Bard is at a major&nbsp;
disadvantage regardless of how good it really is.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.460
GOOGLE’S SECRET SAUCE:
To understand why Bard is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.740
how should I say it flopping by all accounts, we&nbsp;
have to take a look at why other Google products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:45.240
did so well to begin with. Looking back, it’s&nbsp;
actually been quite a while since Google hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.440
a homerun. Google’s glory days were really&nbsp;
back in the 2000s when they were launching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.800
bangers left and right. They had Android in&nbsp;
2008, Chrome also in 2008, YouTube in 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:02.340
Google Maps also in 2005, and Gmail in 2004.&nbsp;
Clearly, they were killing it but by all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.800
accounts they shouldn’t have been. Gmail came out&nbsp;
in an era when Yahoo mail and Hotmail had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:13.380
won over the market. And contrary to popular&nbsp;
belief, YouTube is not the OG video sharing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.900
platform. Both Vimeo and Dailymotion actually&nbsp;
came out before YouTube. And as for Chrome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:24.480
well Chrome came out during a time when Internet&nbsp;
Explorer was ubiquitous. So, Google should’ve had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.600
no chance yet they were able to rise above the big&nbsp;
tech companies of the era Microsoft and Yahoo. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.940
what gave Google the edge? Well, one of the&nbsp;
biggest factors that propelled Google forward was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.920
the fact that they were the scrappy startup trying&nbsp;
to take on these big tech companies. And much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.600
Google’s success actually didn’t have to do with&nbsp;
how good Google’s products were and more to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.880
with how much people hated Microsoft. This might&nbsp;
be hard to imagine today given that Microsoft has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.300
largely faded into the background out of the&nbsp;
public spotlight but back in the early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:00.240
Microsoft was the most highly contested company&nbsp;
in the world. There was literally a supreme court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.620
case regarding whether Microsoft should be broken&nbsp;
up into 2 different companies. Microsoft would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.000
barely escape this fate by the skin of their&nbsp;
teeth but the same could not be same about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.500
public perception. The fact that Microsoft was&nbsp;
able to monopolize the computer space and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.360
away with it made people outraged. This was&nbsp;
one of the main selling factors of Firefox.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.580
The idea was that people would be more willing to&nbsp;
support an open source project as opposed to a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.560
tech offering and they were right. Up until&nbsp;
Chrome came out, Firefox was rapidly eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.000
into Internet Explorer’s marketshare peaking at&nbsp;
over 30% marketshare. Something else to note is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.160
that virtually all of Google’s most successful&nbsp;
products were copycat products. A search engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:45.240
not new. An email, not new. A browser, not new.&nbsp;
And as for the Google office suite, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.860
do I even have to say anything? Now, Google did&nbsp;
make these spaces better with their offerings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:55.200
Google search made it easier to find things.&nbsp;
Gmail gave more storage. Chrome was way more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:00.900
reliable. And collaboration with Google Office&nbsp;
was light years ahead of Microsoft Office. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:06.420
wait a minute, isn’t Google trying to do the same&nbsp;
thing with Bard. Sure, they copy pasted ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:12.120
but they’re trying to make it better in terms&nbsp;
of UI, live info, and better integration? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:17.700
yeah, but people aren’t impressed given Google’s&nbsp;
resources. The bottomline is that people used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:22.380
resonate with Google’s slogan of “Don’t be Evil”&nbsp;
during a time when people felt that big tech and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.060
especially Microsoft was evil. This perception&nbsp;
hasn’t changed all that much over the years but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:32.880
Google’s position within the totem pole has. They&nbsp;
are quote on quote evil big tech company now and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.220
people are just naturally inclined to embrace&nbsp;
the underdog which in this case is ChatGPT.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.060
GOOGLE’S LOST MOJO:&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.260
Hearing all this, you might be inclined to&nbsp;
believe that the deterioration of Google’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:51.720
brand is more of a recent trend but that’s not&nbsp;
the case. In fact, Google has been fighting this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.580
uphill battle for over a decade at this point and&nbsp;
they’ve actually been losing for quite some time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:03.240
The first example of this trend dates back to 2011&nbsp;
with Google+. If you’re not familiar with Google+,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.740
it was basically Google’s attempt at creating&nbsp;
a social media platform and taking on Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:15.900
but it ended up flopping massively. Why? Well,&nbsp;
for starters, 2011 was clearly not too late to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.860
launch a social media platform as we’ve seen&nbsp;
massively successful platforms being launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.640
way after, so timing wasn’t the problem. Many&nbsp;
reports like to claim it was Google+’s lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.040
distinguishing factors and lack luster UI that led&nbsp;
to it’s eventual failure but really the number one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.260
factor that destroyed Google+ was the fact that&nbsp;
Google was pushing it down people’s throats. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:38.580
figured that since they already had a bunch of&nbsp;
users, they no longer had to convince these users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:42.840
based on the merit of their platform. Instead,&nbsp;
they could just force their existing YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:48.900
users to become Google+ users as well. With that,&nbsp;
Google made it required to have a Google+ account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:54.660
to make comments on YouTube. This ended up being&nbsp;
one of Google’s most controversial decisions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:00.480
all time. Not only did millions of YouTube users&nbsp;
rise up against Google but even a cofounder of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.880
YouTube rose up, Jawed Karim. After 8 years of not&nbsp;
posting anything on his YouTube account, he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:11.220
post: why the f do I need a Google+ account to&nbsp;
comment on a video. After taking this much heat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:15.720
Google would of course roll back the Google+&nbsp;
integration with YouTube but by this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:21.360
the reputation of Google+ was already toast.&nbsp;
For the first time, people felt that Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:27.420
had become the villain. Bundling Google+ in with&nbsp;
YouTube was very much a Microsoft move. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.920
Google was by no means the underdog within&nbsp;
the social media scene. You had Instagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.560
and Snapchat and WhatsApp and even Facebook who&nbsp;
were all much younger than Google. But it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.940
just younger companies that Google was losing&nbsp;
to either. Google was also very much losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:46.740
to established companies as well. Likely the&nbsp;
best example is Google Meet which was trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.540
to take on Skype and Webex. Again, Google&nbsp;
Meet didn’t exactly flop just because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.860
was late to the game. Just think about how big&nbsp;
of an opportunity they had during the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:02.100
but no one chose Google Meet. Everyone chose&nbsp;
Zoom or Teams. The reason being that Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:06.960
Meet really didn’t add anything to the video&nbsp;
conferencing space other than the fact that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.040
was from Google. Google Meet just felt like&nbsp;
a product that was meant to expand Google’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.420
reach and control as opposed to something that&nbsp;
really improved video conferencing. If anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:20.040
it made video conferencing worse as it was&nbsp;
browser based which came along with a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:25.320
shortcomings. And those are just two examples.&nbsp;
Some other massive flops from Google over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:31.080
past decade include Google Buzz, Google Tango,&nbsp;
Google Daydream, Google Allo, Google Trips, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:36.300
Google Stadia just to name a few. So, as you can&nbsp;
see, Google has struggled to launch a new product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:41.880
for quite some time now both against startups&nbsp;
and established giants but it get’s even worse.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:42.480
SELF SABOTAGE:&nbsp;

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.100
Not only has Google struggled to launch new&nbsp;
successful products but they have also been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.060
putting off existing users by making&nbsp;
a slew of controversial decisions. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:58.200
example, with YouTube, they removed the dislike&nbsp;
button despite the community being largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:04.440
against it. Similarly, more and more Google search&nbsp;
results seem to be dominated by ads. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:10.380
these aren’t ground breaking developments but each&nbsp;
and every one of these choices make users just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:15.120
little bit more frustrated with Google. Also,&nbsp;
we can’t talk about Google’s shortfalls without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:20.160
talking about their privacy and data collection&nbsp;
concerns. To be honest, I think most people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:25.260
simply come to accept that less privacy is simply&nbsp;
how the modern world works but that doesn’t mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.940
that people are exactly happy with Google and&nbsp;
Facebook which brings us into the launch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.800
Google Bard. Just by it’s initial response,&nbsp;
you can tell that people are predispositioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:39.960
to find it unappealing. This isn’t to say that&nbsp;
Google will necessarily shut down Bard. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:45.480
you can bet that that’s the last thing they’ll do&nbsp;
as they try to make it into the AI space. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.880
doesn’t exactly mean that Bard will be successful.&nbsp;
Sure, a good number of people will always use it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:56.400
just because of how popular Google is as a company&nbsp;
but Google will have a pretty tough time making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:02.400
Bard the dominant language model. Instead, it’s&nbsp;
likely that Bard will end up like Bing and Edge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.560
It didn’t matter how good these products were or&nbsp;
what incentives they offered. Edge has been faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:13.020
than Chrome for years and Bing has literally&nbsp;
been paying users to use it. But despite this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:18.480
everyone continued to use Chrome and Google and&nbsp;
treated Edge and Bing as second tier products.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:24.420
And judging by public perception, it seems that&nbsp;
a very similar fate is up ahead not just for Bard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:29.700
but any consumer oriented product that Google&nbsp;
launches in the imminent future. Likely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:36.060
only way to combat this perception is time. Back&nbsp;
in the late 2000s, Microsoft was faced with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:40.560
same dilemma and everything they launched flopped&nbsp;
regardless of how good it was whether that’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:45.600
Zune or the Windows phone. The way that Microsoft&nbsp;
overcame this dilemma was stepping out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.580
spotlight, focusing on the enterprise business,&nbsp;
and letting people find a new Microsoft to hate on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:55.740
which was Google and Facebook. And suddenly people&nbsp;
are willing to give Micorsoft a chance again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:01.680
So, the answer for Google may be to also step back&nbsp;
and focus on their enterprise cloud business and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.080
let people find a new Google to hate on. This&nbsp;
would give Google the best shot of success in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:12.480
the consumer space becuase the truth with Bard&nbsp;
is that doesn't matter how good it is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:17.940
people have already made up their minds about&nbsp;
Bard. And that why’s Google Bard doesn’t stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:23.340
a chance even if it’s better than ChatGPT.&nbsp;
Things aren’t super rosy at OpenAI either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:27.960
as they’re very much going through a funding&nbsp;
crisis. Check out this video to learn more.

